---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import PlateHeader from '../../components/PlateHeader.astro';
import InterviewGlowUpWorkspace from '../../components/interview-glow-up/InterviewGlowUpWorkspace';
import ErrorBoundary from '../../components/shared/ErrorBoundary';
import GuideCoach from '../../components/shared/GuideCoach';
---

<Base
  title="Interview Glow Up · Workspace"
  description="Decode a job posting, write interview stories you can prove, keep a library of them, and build a packet with a one-screen cheat sheet for the call."
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Interview Glow Up', href: '/interview-glow-up/' },
    { label: 'Workspace', href: '/interview-glow-up/workspace/' },
  ]}
  structuredData={[]}
>
  <div class="relative space-y-10 overflow-hidden px-4 pb-16 pt-4 sm:pt-6">
    <PlateHeader
      tag="Plate IV · Prepare"
      roman="IV"
      title="Interview Workspace"
      description="Decode the job posting, write stories that prove the skills it tests, then pack your best ones for the call."
      accent="love"
    />
    <GuideCoach client:load tool="interview-glow-up" />
    <section class="mx-auto min-h-[70svh] w-full max-w-7xl">
      <ErrorBoundary client:load>
        <InterviewGlowUpWorkspace client:load />
      </ErrorBoundary>
    </section>
    <section class="mx-auto grid w-full max-w-6xl gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
      <article
        class="rounded-3xl border border-border/35 bg-overlay/25 p-6"
      >
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-foreground">Decode the job</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Start with the job posting: what it requires, what is nice to have, the duties, and the
          tools it names. That keeps your preparation tied to the job you actually applied for.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article
        class="rounded-3xl border border-border/35 bg-overlay/25 p-6"
      >
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-foreground">Write your stories</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          Add true examples of what you did and what came of it. Each story is tagged with the
          skills it shows, so you are not searching your memory during the conversation.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article
        class="rounded-3xl border border-border/35 bg-overlay/25 p-6"
      >
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-foreground">Use the packet</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground">
          The packet is meant to be short. Keep your strongest stories, cut the rest, and use the
          HUD, a one-screen cheat sheet for the call, as a calm reminder of what you want to say.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>
    <section
      class="mx-auto w-full max-w-4xl space-y-4 text-base leading-relaxed text-muted-foreground"
    >
      <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-foreground">How to work the packet</h2>
      <p>
        Treat the workspace like a practice bench. Paste the job posting, see what it tests, attach
        stories with real results, and leave weaker examples out of the packet. By the end you are
        working from things you can point to.
      </p>
      <p>
        After the first pass, read the packet as the interviewer would. Each story should make the
        situation, what you did, and what came of it easy to follow without extra explanation. If a
        story feels vague, go back to the Library and add a clearer result or a better example
        before the call. Then practice saying them out loud in the rehearsal room.
      </p>
      <p>A smaller packet is easier to remember under pressure.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
